We would like to express our sincere gratitude to all reviewers for their insightful and constructive feedback. We are encouraged by the following recognitions:
* Novelty and Design Rationale: Our PairUni framework was praised as "elegant and logically sound" (p31U) and "novel" (aZhL). Reviewer jd4Y commended the "simple, implementable" Pair-GRPO mechanism and the "pragmatic" dataset construction.
* Effectiveness and Generalizability: Reviewers noted "performance gains across a comprehensive suite" of benchmarks (cpEf) and "consistent and balanced" improvements (p31U). The method's effectiveness across diverse architectures and scales was also highlighted (cpEf, jd4Y).

We have tried our best to revise the paper to address all concerns. All revisions are marked in purple. Specifically:

@Reviewers cpef and p31U, we have scaled our method to **diverse architectures** (Lumina-DiMOO and Bagel) and refined the corresponding experiments in Appendix B. These experiments demonstrate the clear scalability and generalizability of our unified training paradigm across different model sizes and tasks.

@Reviewer p31U, we have extended our evaluations to **image editing benchmarks**, where our method achieves consistent improvements without using any specific editing training data.

@Reviewers cpef and jd4Y, we have added **extensive ablations** on weighting strategies, similarity thresholds, and feature extractors. The results confirm the robustness of our design choices and isolate the specific contributions of the Pair-GPRO algorithm.

@Reviewers cpef and jd4Y, we have provided further **analysis on gradient conflict patterns** and disentangled the contributions of data pairing versus data quality.
